by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-31T16:43:42Z

Thank you for the patience. I rebased and reworked the patch, here is what changed and why.

The submitted version forced `'headers' => []`, and `Serializer::decode()` rejects empty headers before it looks at anything else, so the new path could never work with Symfony's own serializer. Worse, when the producer did send a `headers` field it was dropped. Checked end to end against a real Redis with the stock serializer:

| `XADD` fields | 8.2 | PR as submitted | after rework |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `message '{"body":…,"headers":{"type":…}}'` | consumed | consumed | consumed |
| `body '<json>'` | silently dropped | decode failed | decode failed |
| `body '<json>' headers '{"type":…}'` | silently dropped | decode failed | **consumed** |

The third row is the one that makes the feature worth having, so `headers` is now read from the stream field and JSON-decoded, falling back to `[]` when it is absent or not a JSON object.

Also:

* Added the tests for the `headers` field on `get()` and `find()`, one for a `headers` field that is not JSON, and an integration test that consumes a raw `XADD` through the real `Serializer`. All seven new tests fail against the unpatched receiver.
* Dropped the `BODY_FIELD` / `HEADERS_FIELD` / `MESSAGE_FIELD` constants. They accounted for most of the diff, and `Connection` writes the same three literals on the producer side, so the class was ending up half and half.
* Reworded the changelog entry to name the accepted fields.

One thing to know for the first row: `XADD messages '*' message '{"body":"…","headers":{"type":"App\\Message\\Mail"}}'` already worked before this pull request, with no custom serializer. It is undocumented, which is why nobody finds it. The description now lists what the documentation pull request has to cover.

The `is_array()` guard you added is a real bug fix on its own: `json_decode()` returns a scalar for a `message` field holding `12345`, the `null ===` guard lets it through and `array_key_exists()` raises an uncaught `TypeError` that kills the worker. That one goes back to 6.4, so I sent it separately in #65779.

